  <div class="views-field views-field-body">        <div class="field-content"><a href="/web/20160623003101/http://www.educationworld.com/a_lesson/summer-science-experiments-students.shtml">Ten Super Summer Science Activities

Hoping to avoid the summer slide? Science activities, projects and experiments are perfect for keeping kids' brains engaged during the school break.

Check out these cool summer science activities for students of all ages:

Sticky Ice: Turn a piece of ice into a sticky, glue-like substance. This experiment can be done outdoors and only takes a few ingredients: water, an ice cube, a piece of string and salt. Kids will learn about salt's effect on water and cool off in the process!
	Exploding Lunch Bag: This experiment will wow children as they see the result of a chemical reaction between vinegar and baking soda. \xc2\xa0
	Lemony Eruptions: With simple household ingredients, students can conduct this fun, hands-on experiment with ease. Combine learning, sensory and fine-motor skills and have fun touching the colorful super foam.\xc2\xa0
	Tie Dye: Tie dye is always a crowd favorite and afterwards, kids can incorporate the results into their wardrobe!<img alt="" height="157" src="/web/20160623003101im_/http://www.educationworld.com/sites/default/files/lightning_story.jpg" style="border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; margin: 11px 10px; float: right;" width="212"/>
	Make Lightning: With a pencil, aluminum tray, wool cloth, styrofoam plate and thumb tack, kids learn how static electricity and a metal component can create a spark!
	Grow and Shrink Marshmallows: Use a kitchen vacuum packer and watch the favorite s'mores ingredient grow and shrink before your eyes.\xc2\xa0
	Anemometer: Teach about how meteorologists measure wind speed by creating your own anemometer. Just wait for a windy day and keep track of how much the cups spin.\xc2\xa0
	Rocks and Minerals Scratch Test: Teach and study the cycle of rocks with this easy scratch test. Read up on rocks and minerals and then get to work! All you need is a collection of rocks, black and white construction paper and a magnifying glass.\xc2\xa0
	Rainbow Bubble Snakes: With an empty water bottle, duct tape, an old sock, dish soap and food coloring, students can make a colorful stream of bubbles--or bubble snake--form like magic.
	Foam Dough: Kids love crafting objects from play dough. With just shaving cream and corn starch, they can create their own moldable dough. Add food coloring for more fun!
Related resource

Show-Biz Science


Article by Kassondra Granata, EducationWorld ContributorEducation World\xc2\xae\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0\xc2\xa0
Copyright \xc2\xa9 2014 Education World</a></div>  </div>  
  <div class="views-field views-field-title">        <span class="field-content"><a href="/web/20160623003101/http://www.educationworld.com/a_lesson/summer-science-experiments-students.shtml">Ten Summer Science Activities for Kids</a></span>  </div>  </div>
  <div class="views-row views-row-3 views-row-odd views-row-last">

  <div class="views-field views-field-body">        <div class="field-content"><a href="/web/20160623003101/http://www.educationworld.com/a_news/search-validation-494088191"><img alt="" height="195" src="/web/20160623003101im_/http://www.educationworld.com/sites/default/files/juggler.jpg" width="450"/>

None of us got into the field of education for the accolades.\xc2\xa0 Although we each certainly have our separate reasons, admiration and praise was never on the agenda, and the majority of us would have it no other way.\xc2\xa0 Teaching, as they say, is its own reward.

Still, every professional needs positive feedback and validation.\xc2\xa0 Not that anyone wants to sit around patting themselves on the back, but our brain\xe2\x80\x99s reward system serves a very real function.\xc2\xa0 And yet, there\xe2\x80\x99s something about the field (or perhaps the personality types that choose to enter the field) that makes it hard to feel good about our own growth and progress.\xc2\xa0 And we need that reassurance, according to a recent study done by the Harvard Business School.\xc2\xa0 In the study, participants visited the Harvard Decision Science Laboratory and were asked to solve a variety of problems.\xc2\xa0 Half the participants, however, were asked to have friends and family members send them an email prior to the study that described \xe2\x80\x9ca time when the participant was at his or her best.\xe2\x80\x9d\xc2\xa0 After reading the email, they were off to work on the problem-solving tasks.\xc2\xa0 According to Dr. Jooa Julia Lee, one of the co-authors of the study, \xe2\x80\x9cOverwhelmingly, those who read positive statements about their past actions were more creative in their approach, more successful at problem-solving and less stressed out than their counterparts.\xe2\x80\x9d\xc2\xa0 As in any other workplace, educators need to know what they are doing right, need to feel confident in their progress, and hopeful for future professional endeavors.\xc2\xa0 But in our current system, there are a lot of barriers to this sort of support.

The Data Dilemma

Here\xe2\x80\x99s the thing:\xc2\xa0 Education\xe2\x80\x94across our world\xe2\x80\x94is becoming more and more data-driven.\xc2\xa0 And the truth is, mostly, it\xe2\x80\x99s pretty darn useful.\xc2\xa0 We have more information about our students than we ever have in history, mapping skills, behaviors, and challenges from birth to graduation and beyond.\xc2\xa0 Everything is quantifiable.\xc2\xa0 Ah, but herein lies the discomfort.\xc2\xa0 Do these numbers and bar graphs really reflect the impact we have on our students?\xc2\xa0 It is a full snapshot of what we actually do as educators?\xc2\xa0 Of course not.\xc2\xa0 Yet, with teacher performance more often being tied to achievement data, it\xe2\x80\x99s hard to stay upbeat.\xc2\xa0 For every gain you want to feel good about, there\xe2\x80\x99s a standard you might not be meeting.\xc2\xa0 For every smile you can elicit in an assignment, there\xe2\x80\x99s an achievement gap staring you down.\xc2\xa0 Can we (and should we) really be measuring our own success and self-worth on statistics and testing?\xc2\xa0 Should you be happy about helping three struggling students construct their first MLA-formatted essay, or sad because they didn\xe2\x80\x99t meet their benchmark assessment goals this semester?\xc2\xa0 Which of these makes you a \xe2\x80\x9cgood\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9cbad\xe2\x80\x9d teacher?\xc2\xa0 The latter is all that shows up on your end-of-the-year evaluation.\xc2\xa0 All you're held accountable for.\xc2\xa0 But the former matters.\xc2\xa0 It must.

Smile-Driven Accountability Measures

Seldom will students go out of their way to celebrate you in the classroom.\xc2\xa0 And it\xe2\x80\x99s not their job to do so.\xc2\xa0 It\xe2\x80\x99s a rare moon to have a student approach you with a firm handshake, a tear in her eye, as she thanks you profusely for that lesson on diagramming sentences or order of operations.\xc2\xa0 Every once in a while, we get those golden moments, sure.\xc2\xa0 The excitement in a student\xe2\x80\x99s eyes as a concept clarifies.\xc2\xa0 The joy of watching exquisitely-done performance tasks.\xc2\xa0 A \xe2\x80\x9cthanks for understanding\xe2\x80\x9d.\xc2\xa0 And yes, even the elusive \xe2\x80\x9cthat was cool, mister\xe2\x80\x9d or \xe2\x80\x9ctoday was fun, miss\xe2\x80\x9d.\xc2\xa0 These are the moments\xe2\x80\x94even few and far between\xe2\x80\x94that remind us why we do what we do.\xc2\xa0 Are these the instruments with which we shall use to gauge our successes?\xc2\xa0 It feels more real to me than correlated numbers, but is it enough?

That \xe2\x80\x9cNever Good Enough\xe2\x80\x9d Mentality of Ours

Educators are constantly dancing on the edge of Occam\xe2\x80\x99s razor.\xc2\xa0 A million variables, relying on the simplest or most rational explanations to work with, because we need to act tomorrow morning.\xc2\xa0 Not to mention the constant reflection, sorting through those variables:\xc2\xa0 home life, emotional distress, intellectual struggles, equity, and socioeconomics.\xc2\xa0 One statement remains true:\xc2\xa0 Our students deserve the best.\xc2\xa0 Every day, educators strive to do better, better, better.\xc2\xa0 More engagement.\xc2\xa0 More differentiation.\xc2\xa0 More relevance.\xc2\xa0 So, innately, the job entails at least some level of \xe2\x80\x9cnever good enough\xe2\x80\x9d.\xc2\xa0 If our students deserve the world, how could we ever be enough?\xc2\xa0 It\xe2\x80\x99s an admirable belief system, and an incredible motivator for the lifelong learner/educator.\xc2\xa0 And yet, far from healthy.

What can we do?

You want to be the best teacher.\xc2\xa0 In theory, this will make you \xe2\x80\x9chappy\xe2\x80\x9d, no?\xc2\xa0 This will satisfy your need to serve.\xc2\xa0 This will give justice to what our students\xe2\x80\x99 generation deserves.\xc2\xa0 But how can we quantify this?\xc2\xa0 We meticulously map student progress\xe2\x80\xa6How can we fairly and honestly map our own progress?\xc2\xa0 How can we ever feel like we\xe2\x80\x99re doing the good work we set out to do all those years ago when we decided to take up the honor or teaching?\xc2\xa0 Believe it or not, it starts with the small stuff.

1.\xc2\xa0 Keep a Sunshine Journal

In a study published in Current Directions in Psychological Science, a journal of the Association for Psychological Science, Boston College psychologist, Elizabeth Kensinger and colleagues explain how \xe2\x80\x9cnegative events [are] remembered in greater detail than positive ones.\xe2\x80\x9d\xc2\xa0 And this actually makes a lot of sense, evolutionarily, no?\xc2\xa0 According to Kensinger, \xe2\x80\x9cIt is logical that attention would be focused on potentially threatening information.\xe2\x80\x9d

Unfortunately, this means that for every ten smiles in your classroom, every ten \xe2\x80\x9caha\xe2\x80\x9d moments, every ten skills finally mastered, you will only remember the one student that bombed your post-test.\xc2\xa0 The student who won\xe2\x80\x99t connect.\xc2\xa0 The student who doesn\xe2\x80\x99t show up.

You can change this.\xc2\xa0 Keep a personal journal for diligent recording of the \xe2\x80\x9cgood days\xe2\x80\x9d and moments of virtue in the classroom.\xc2\xa0 Each entry doesn\xe2\x80\x99t have to be an essay\xe2\x80\x94just noted for posterity.\xc2\xa0 According to a study published in the Journal of Research in Personality, writing about intensely positive experiences can lead to \xe2\x80\x9cbetter mood levels, fewer visits to the health center\xe2\x80\x9d\xe2\x80\x94and even less overall illness.\xc2\xa0 And writing improves memory.\xc2\xa0 So, if you\xe2\x80\x99re consciously recording the joys of your work, you\xe2\x80\x99re much more likely to reflect upon your work joyously.\xc2\xa0 And if we\xe2\x80\x99re to believe our memories are, indeed, biased toward our failures rather than our successes, such a journal would only serve as a more realistic portrait of the work we do.

2.\xc2\xa0 Social Media for Grads

This suggestion is pretty controversial for schools and districts that have strict policies against it.\xc2\xa0 By no means are we suggesting you should go against your community\xe2\x80\x99s recommendations.\xc2\xa0 Engaging in social media can be a risky business for teachers, so understand the full implications of that risk.\xc2\xa0 Having said that, keeping a Facebook, Twitter, Instagram, or other social media account to stay in contact with recent grads can be a wonderful thing.\xc2\xa0 Set up a separate \xe2\x80\x9cteacher\xe2\x80\x9d account that is completely separate from any personal accounts you might have already.\xc2\xa0 And ne\xe2\x80\x99er the two shall meet.\xc2\xa0 Having former students as \xe2\x80\x9cfriends\xe2\x80\x9d in this context has allowed me to see them grow, struggle, and overcome.\xc2\xa0 Watching students apply for jobs and schools, starting families of their own, and continuing to encourage and support them a decade, two decades, past their graduation date can be extremely rewarding.\xc2\xa0 Yes, that ninth grader did everything they could to distract your World History course.\xc2\xa0 But now he\xe2\x80\x99s a dad.\xc2\xa0 He\xe2\x80\x99s an active member of the community.\xc2\xa0 He\xe2\x80\x99s a hard-working employee.\xc2\xa0 And you helped.\xc2\xa0 Even just a little bit.\xc2\xa0 Maturity does occur.\xc2\xa0 We just don\xe2\x80\x99t always get to see it.\xc2\xa0 Social media can help us witness that growth.\xc2\xa0 Be careful, though.\xc2\xa0 Think about your 20\xe2\x80\x99s.\xc2\xa0 Think about college.\xc2\xa0 When you choose to connect in this way, you\xe2\x80\x99re going to see things you wish you hadn\xe2\x80\x99t.\xc2\xa0 Personally, I\xe2\x80\x99ve found it to be worth it.\xc2\xa0 If your school has very specific restrictions against such interaction\xe2\x80\x94or you have personal reservations\xe2\x80\x94think about the potential of creating a school-sanctioned official \xe2\x80\x9calumni\xe2\x80\x9d page.

3.\xc2\xa0 Staff Celebrations

We spend the entire year celebrating the achievements of our incredible students.\xc2\xa0 Why?\xc2\xa0 Because they deserve it.\xc2\xa0 Because they have worked hard, overcome obstacles, and succeeded.\xc2\xa0 We wouldn\xe2\x80\x99t hesitate to recognize a student\xe2\x80\x99s generosity, ownership, or curiosity. \xc2\xa0Why wouldn\xe2\x80\x99t we extend that courtesy to our peers?\xc2\xa0 After all, schools are communities.\xc2\xa0 And staff are a part of that community.\xc2\xa0 They, too, deserve to be recognized.\xc2\xa0 Encouraged.\xc2\xa0 Consider starting your school, department, or data team meetings with \xe2\x80\x9ccelebrations\xe2\x80\x9d.\xc2\xa0 It\xe2\x80\x99s a great way to take a moment to be mindfully positive and uplifting about our work.\xc2\xa0 Instead of ruminating about what we still need to do, should be doing, should have done, take a moment to recognize and really own what we have achieved.\xc2\xa0 Were last week\xe2\x80\x99s presentations particularly impressive?\xc2\xa0 Did a lesson go better than expected?\xc2\xa0 Did you somehow manage to get Craig to lift his head off the table and engage for the first time all month?\xc2\xa0 Phenomenal.\xc2\xa0 Own that.\xc2\xa0 Share that.\xc2\xa0 You\xe2\x80\x99d be surprised by how quickly it\xe2\x80\x99ll change the tone of the meeting.\xc2\xa0 Teachers need to feel like they\xe2\x80\x99re not alone in their struggle.\xc2\xa0 That they\xe2\x80\x99re not in competition with each other.\xc2\xa0 That they are all working together, on the same team, toward a goal and a dream.\xc2\xa0 Staff celebrations can encourage solidarity in that noble mission.

4.\xc2\xa0 Find Your Workplace Equilibrium

On the note of solidarity generating a sense of community well-being and celebration of success, there is also the very compelling opportunity to use that community to vent about the challenges and frustrations of the job.\xc2\xa0 This is the infamous \xe2\x80\x9cnew teacher tip\xe2\x80\x9d of avoiding the teacher lounge.\xc2\xa0 It\xe2\x80\x99s not to say all venting is a bad thing.\xc2\xa0 To know one is not alone in the trials and tribulations of our work with students can be the saving grace to an otherwise disastrous week.\xc2\xa0 Having a peer who understands, someone to lean on and be honest with in times of turmoil\xe2\x80\x94this is an absolute necessity in any field.\xc2\xa0 They key here\xe2\x80\xa6is to find balance.\xc2\xa0 Your celebrations should at very least match your grievances.

This is no easy task!\xc2\xa0 The spiral of negative commiserating tantalizes even the veteran educators.\xc2\xa0 And that\xe2\x80\x99s okay.\xc2\xa0 It\xe2\x80\x99s a difficult job.\xc2\xa0 Any field dealing directly with human beings is subject to unexpected variability and overwhelming emotional drains.\xc2\xa0 It makes sense that we\xe2\x80\x99d dwell on such a prominent aspect of the work we do.\xc2\xa0 But there\xe2\x80\x99s a line between healthy community and the undertow of burnout in this respect.\xc2\xa0 Notice when you are taking on someone else\xe2\x80\x99s emotional state.\xc2\xa0 Be mindful of who you are choosing to associate with in the building\xe2\x80\x94mix it up!\xc2\xa0 Surrounding yourself with encouragement will do wonders for your own workplace satisfaction (especially when you recognize that you\xe2\x80\x99d much rather be stewing with the pity party). Never leave the day without identifying (and sharing) one success. Recognizing and owning your very-human tendency toward negativity, and mindfully working to respect and balance it, will give you a much more honest and accurate outlook on the great work you do.

\xc2\xa0

Written by Keith Lambert, Education World Associate Contributing Editor

Lambert is an English / Language Arts teacher and teacher trainer in Connecticut.</a></div>  </div>  
